# `A ⊴ O_p(U)` for the `(p,p)`-subgroup `A` of §4 (8)

Bender 1981, §4 (8), p. 226: with `Q := Ω₁(Z₂(O_p(U)))` (`Qsub`) and `A` the subgroup of type
`(p,p)` of `F × K₀` lying in `Q`, the subgroup `A` is normal in `O_p(U)` (`pCoreIn p B.U`).

The mathematical content of this file is the general fact

  `[Ω₁(Z₂(P)), P] ≤ Z(Ω₁(Z₂(P)))`      for `P` any subgroup of a group,

proved here as `commutator_omega1_z2_mem_centerIn` (pointwise form).  Its proof has two steps:

* `Z₂(P) ⊆ {x ∈ P | [x,P] ≤ Z(P)}` (closure of the "second centre" set,
  `z2_commutator_mem_centerIn`), so that for `x ∈ Z₂(P)` the map `y ↦ [x,y]` lands in `Z(P)`;
  for `x ∈ Ω₁(Z₂(P))`, i.e. `x` is generated by elements `g` of `Z₂(P)` with `g ^ p = 1`,
  the identity `[g,y] ^ p = [g ^ p, y]` (`Commute.commutatorElement_pow_left`, applicable because
  `[g,y]` commutes with `g`) gives `[g,y] ^ p = 1`, and `[g,y] ∈ Z(P) ⊆ Z₂(P)`, so
  `[g,y] ∈ Ω₁(Z₂(P))`; being central in `P ⊇ Ω₁(Z₂(P))`, it lies in the centre of
  `Ω₁(Z₂(P))`.  The set of such `x` is a subgroup, and the closure induction
  (`Subgroup.closure_induction`) propagates the property to all of `Ω₁(Z₂(P))`.

The assembly then only needs the two trivialities `Ω₁(Z₂(P)) ≤ P` and `Z(Q) ≤ Q`: if `A ≤ Q` and
`Z(Q) ≤ A`, then for `x ∈ A`, `y ∈ P` one has `y * x * y⁻¹ = [y,x] * x` with
`[y,x] = [x,y]⁻¹ ∈ Z(Q) ≤ A`, hence `A ⊴ P` (`Subgroup.normal_subgroupOf_iff`).

The hypotheses of the statement (`p` prime, and `A = Q` or `Z(Q) < A`) are used only to know
`Z(Q) ≤ A`; neither primality nor finiteness plays a role in the argument.
-/
import Mathlib
import Submission.Work.CORE4.EightIdxDefs
import Submission.Work.CORE4.Defs
import Submission.Work.CORE4.TrichCore

open scoped commutatorElement

namespace Work.CORE4

variable {G : Type} [Group G]

/-- Membership in `centerIn P` (`= Z(P)` viewed in the ambient group): an element of `P`
commuting with every element of `P`. -/
lemma mem_centerIn_iff {P : Subgroup G} {x : G} :
    x ∈ centerIn P ↔ x ∈ P ∧ ∀ y ∈ P, x * y = y * x := by
  constructor
  · intro hx
    obtain ⟨z, hz, hzx⟩ := Subgroup.mem_map.mp hx
    subst hzx
    refine ⟨z.2, fun y hy => ?_⟩
    have h := congrArg Subtype.val ((Subgroup.mem_center_iff (G := ↥P)).mp hz ⟨y, hy⟩)
    exact h.symm
  · rintro ⟨hxP, hx⟩
    refine Subgroup.mem_map.mpr ⟨⟨x, hxP⟩, ?_, rfl⟩
    exact (Subgroup.mem_center_iff (G := ↥P)).mpr fun g => (Subtype.ext (hx ↑g g.2)).symm


/-- The centre of a subgroup, viewed in the ambient group, lies in it. -/
lemma centerIn_le_self (Q : Subgroup G) : centerIn Q ≤ Q :=
  fun _ hx => (mem_centerIn_iff.mp hx).1

/-- `Z(P) ≤ Z₂(P)`: a central element of `P` commutes with every commutator. -/
lemma centerIn_le_z2 (P : Subgroup G) : centerIn P ≤ z2 P := by
  intro z hz
  obtain ⟨hzP, hzc⟩ := mem_centerIn_iff.mp hz
  refine Subgroup.subset_closure ⟨hzP, fun y hy w hw => ?_⟩
  have h1 : ⁅z, y⁆ = 1 := commutatorElement_eq_one_iff_mul_comm.mpr (hzc y hy)
  rw [h1, commutatorElement_one_left]

/-- **`Z₂(P) ⊆ {x ∈ P | [x,P] ≤ Z(P)}`**, pointwise: the commutator of an element of `Z₂(P)`
with an element of `P` is central in `P`.  (Both sides are `Z(P)`-valued; the statement is the
closure-clearing step of the second-centre set `z2Set P`.) -/
lemma z2_commutator_mem_centerIn (P : Subgroup G) {x y : G} (hx : x ∈ z2 P) (hy : y ∈ P) :
    ⁅x, y⁆ ∈ centerIn P := by
  induction hx using Subgroup.closure_induction with
  | mem z hz =>
      obtain ⟨hzP, hzc⟩ := hz
      rw [mem_centerIn_iff]
      refine ⟨P.mul_mem (P.mul_mem (P.mul_mem hzP hy) (P.inv_mem hzP)) (P.inv_mem hy), ?_⟩
      intro w hw
      exact commutatorElement_eq_one_iff_mul_comm.mp (hzc y hy w hw)
  | one => rw [commutatorElement_one_left]; exact Subgroup.one_mem _
  | mul a b ha hb iha ihb =>
      rw [commutatorElement_mul_left_eq_conj_mul]
      have haP : a ∈ P := z2_le_self P ha
      have hconj : a * ⁅b, y⁆ * a⁻¹ = ⁅b, y⁆ := by
        have h := (mem_centerIn_iff.mp ihb).2 a haP
        rw [← h, mul_assoc, mul_inv_cancel, mul_one]
      rw [hconj]
      exact (centerIn P).mul_mem ihb iha
  | inv a ha iha =>
      rw [commutatorElement_inv_left]
      have haP : a ∈ P := z2_le_self P ha
      have hmem : ⁅y, a⁆ ∈ centerIn P := by
        have h : ⁅a, y⁆⁻¹ ∈ centerIn P := (centerIn P).inv_mem iha
        rwa [commutatorElement_inv] at h
      have hconj : a⁻¹ * ⁅y, a⁆ * a = ⁅y, a⁆ := by
        have h := (mem_centerIn_iff.mp hmem).2 a haP
        rw [mul_assoc, h, ← mul_assoc, inv_mul_cancel, one_mul]
      rw [hconj]
      exact hmem

/-- **`[Ω₁(Z₂(P)), P] ≤ Z(Ω₁(Z₂(P)))`**, pointwise form.

For `x ∈ Ω₁(Z₂(P))` and `y ∈ P`, the commutator `[x,y]` lies in the centre of `Ω₁(Z₂(P))`:
`[x,y] ∈ Ω₁(Z₂(P))` (closure induction; each generator `g` of `Ω₁(Z₂(P))` satisfies
`[g,y] ∈ Z(P)` with `[g,y] ^ p = [g ^ p, y] = 1`) and `[x,y]` centralises `Ω₁(Z₂(P))` because it
centralises `P` at the generators.

No primality of `p` is used. -/
theorem commutator_omega1_z2_mem_centerIn (p : ℕ) (P : Subgroup G) {x y : G}
    (hx : x ∈ omega1 p (z2 P)) (hy : y ∈ P) :
    ⁅x, y⁆ ∈ centerIn (omega1 p (z2 P)) := by
  have key : ∀ x ∈ (Subgroup.closure (omega1Set p (z2 P)) : Subgroup G),
      ⁅x, y⁆ ∈ centerIn (Subgroup.closure (omega1Set p (z2 P)) : Subgroup G) := by
    intro x hx
    induction hx using Subgroup.closure_induction with
    | mem z hz =>
        obtain ⟨hz2, hzp⟩ := hz
        have hzP : z ∈ P := z2_le_self P hz2
        have hc : ⁅z, y⁆ ∈ centerIn P := z2_commutator_mem_centerIn P hz2 hy
        have hcz2 : ⁅z, y⁆ ∈ z2 P := centerIn_le_z2 P hc
        have hpow : ⁅z, y⁆ ^ p = 1 := by
          have hcomm : Commute z ⁅z, y⁆ := ((mem_centerIn_iff.mp hc).2 z hzP).symm
          rw [Commute.commutatorElement_pow_left hcomm p, hzp, commutatorElement_one_left]
        have hQ : ⁅z, y⁆ ∈ omega1 p (z2 P) := Subgroup.subset_closure ⟨hcz2, hpow⟩
        rw [mem_centerIn_iff]
        exact ⟨hQ, fun w hw => (mem_centerIn_iff.mp hc).2 w (omega1_z2_le_self p P hw)⟩
    | one => rw [commutatorElement_one_left]; exact Subgroup.one_mem _
    | mul a b ha hb iha ihb =>
        rw [commutatorElement_mul_left_eq_conj_mul]
        have hconj : a * ⁅b, y⁆ * a⁻¹ = ⁅b, y⁆ := by
          have h := (mem_centerIn_iff.mp ihb).2 a ha
          rw [← h, mul_assoc, mul_inv_cancel, mul_one]
        rw [hconj]
        exact (centerIn _).mul_mem ihb iha
    | inv a ha iha =>
        rw [commutatorElement_inv_left]
        have hmem : ⁅y, a⁆ ∈ centerIn (Subgroup.closure (omega1Set p (z2 P)) : Subgroup G) := by
          have h : ⁅a, y⁆⁻¹ ∈ centerIn (Subgroup.closure (omega1Set p (z2 P)) : Subgroup G) :=
            (centerIn _).inv_mem iha
          rwa [commutatorElement_inv] at h
        have hconj : a⁻¹ * ⁅y, a⁆ * a = ⁅y, a⁆ := by
          have h := (mem_centerIn_iff.mp hmem).2 a ha
          rw [mul_assoc, h, ← mul_assoc, inv_mul_cancel, one_mul]
        rw [hconj]
        exact hmem
  exact key x hx

/-- **Bender 1981, §4 (8)** (the `A ⊴ O_p(U)` step): if `A ≤ Q := Ω₁(Z₂(O_p(U)))` and `A`
contains the centre of `Q` (in particular if `A = Q`), then `A` is normal in `O_p(U)`.

Indeed `[Q, P] ≤ Z(Q) ≤ A` (`commutator_omega1_z2_mem_centerIn`), so for `x ∈ A` and `y ∈ P`,
`y * x * y⁻¹ = [y,x] * x ∈ A`. -/
theorem EightAnormalPcore_of_Qpos {G : Type} [Group G] [Finite G] {B : Work.GW2.BenderSetup G}
    (p : ℕ) (hp : p.Prime) (A : Subgroup G)
    (hAQ : A ≤ Work.CORE4.Qsub p B)
    (hpos : A = Work.CORE4.Qsub p B ∨ Work.CORE4.centerIn (Work.CORE4.Qsub p B) < A) :
    (A.subgroupOf (Work.GW2.pCoreIn p B.U)).Normal := by
  have hA_le : A ≤ Work.GW2.pCoreIn p B.U := hAQ.trans (omega1_z2_le_self p _)
  rw [Subgroup.normal_subgroupOf_iff hA_le]
  intro x y hxA hy
  have hxQ : x ∈ Qsub p B := hAQ hxA
  have hZA : centerIn (Qsub p B) ≤ A := by
    rcases hpos with h | h
    · subst h
      exact centerIn_le_self _
    · exact le_of_lt h
  have hcomm : ⁅x, y⁆ ∈ A :=
    hZA (commutator_omega1_z2_mem_centerIn p (Work.GW2.pCoreIn p B.U) hxQ hy)
  have hyx : ⁅y, x⁆ ∈ A := by
    have h := A.inv_mem hcomm
    rwa [commutatorElement_inv] at h
  rw [show y * x * y⁻¹ = ⁅y, x⁆ * x from by rw [commutatorElement_def]; group]
  exact A.mul_mem hyx hxA

end Work.CORE4
